# LINE ENDINGS ARE PART OF THIS REPO'S CORRECTNESS, so they are pinned here
# rather than left to each clone's git configuration (2026-08-06).
#
# Git for Windows ships with `core.autocrlf=true` by default. Without this file
# a Windows clone rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout, which is not a
# cosmetic difference here: several specs compare EXACT BYTES. The shared
# `.js`/`.cjs` twins are checked for identical logic bodies, the Node history
# index is proven byte-identical against a recorded oracle, and the terminal
# fixtures are captured pty output that a parser matches against character by
# character. All of those fail on a CRLF checkout, and they fail in a way that
# looks like broken code rather than like a checkout setting.
#
# Measured on a real Windows machine: a fresh clone of the published repo failed
# 112 of 1004 unit tests, and a large share of those were this.

# Normalize everything to LF in the working tree, on every platform. The project
# is developed and gated on Linux, and the daemon, the bin/ scripts and the
# pty layer all assume LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# CAPTURED BYTES. These are recordings of real terminal output, real transcripts
# and real queue state, and a spec pages through some of them by byte offset.
# Marking them binary means git will not touch them under any configuration,
# which is stronger than eol=lf and is what they actually need.
app/test/fixtures/** -text
reference/** -text
*.jsonl -text

# Binary assets, so git never attempts a diff or a conversion.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.node binary
*.wasm binary
